Q: How to use reduce() with gt to compute multiple summary statistics rows? A: <ol>
<li>Using <code>reduce</code> from package {purrr} (but base R <code>Map</code> would do as well):</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>library(purrr)

years &lt;- c(2020, 2025) |&gt; as.character()

years |&gt; 
  reduce(.f = \(my_gt, year){ 
    my_gt |&gt; 
      grand_summary_rows(
        columns = ends_with(year),
        fns = sprintf(&quot;Total ~ sum(%s * %s) / sum(%s)&quot;,
                      paste0(&quot;p&quot;, year),
                      paste0(&quot;w&quot;, year),
                      paste0(&quot;w&quot;, year)
        ) |&gt; as.formula()
      )
  },
  .init = df |&gt; gt(rowname_col = &quot;group&quot;)
  ) |&gt; 
  cols_hide(starts_with(&quot;w&quot;))

</code></pre>
<p>returns:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/0vQF9ZCY.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/0vQF9ZCY.png" alt="using reduce on gt" /></a></p>
<p>Note the use of <code>.init</code> to give <code>reduce</code> an initial <code>gt</code> to successively add <code>grand_summary_rows</code>.</p>
<ol start="2">
<li>Although it's possible to do this on the <code>gt</code> side, I'd still leave data <em>manipulation</em> to data <code>frame</code>s or <code>table</code>s and associated tools and <em>presentation</em> to <code>gt</code>. Here's a corresponding tidyverse approach:</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)

df |&gt; 
  pivot_longer(-group) |&gt; 
  separate_wider_regex(name, patterns = c(type = &quot;[pw]&quot;, year = &quot;.*&quot;)) |&gt; 
  pivot_wider(names_from = &quot;type&quot;) |&gt; 
  mutate(p_weighted = p * prop.table(w), .by = year) |&gt; 
  pivot_wider(id_cols = group,
              names_from = year,
              values_from = c(p, w, p_weighted)
  ) |&gt; 
  gt() |&gt; 
  grand_summary_rows(contains(&quot;weighted&quot;), Total ~ sum(.))
</code></pre>
